## Purpose

Establish a realistic private development site for the Tool Shed website and dashboard on
`sup.local` without changing or interrupting the public `ts.rookaro.com` stack during ordinary
development. Keep development close enough to production to prove deployment, migrations,
authentication, reporter communications, browser access, recovery, and exact-commit promotion.

## Desired Outcome

- Development runs on `sup.local` in a distinct host filesystem tree and Docker Compose project.
- The development stack owns its containers, image tags, network, database, volumes, secrets,
  configuration, generated assets, logs, backups, and port `8443`.
- Linux and Windows development project identities communicate continuously with the development
  dashboard using development-only credentials, workers, and safety schedulers.
- LAN clients use `http://192.168.7.5:8443`; Workpc uses a persistent localhost-only SSH tunnel.
- No development route is exposed by public DNS, Nginx Proxy Manager, or the Rookaro router.
- Promotion uses the exact commit proven in development and retains production backup, migration,
  health, and rollback evidence.

## Selected Architecture

| Surface | Development contract | Production boundary |
| --- | --- | --- |
| Host | `sup.local` / `192.168.7.5`, shared Docker daemon | Host and daemon restarts remain shared-risk events |
| Filesystem | A dedicated root selected during implementation | Never reuse `/home/jon/docker/ts.rookaro.com` or its writable paths |
| Compose | Distinct project, working name `tsrookarocom-dev` | Never run unscoped Compose commands against `tsrookarocom` |
| Services | Separate Nginx/docs, Django dashboard, and PostgreSQL containers | No production container, network, volume, secret, or database reuse |
| Access | Plain HTTP on LAN-only port `8443` | No TLS, public DNS, NPM entry, or public router path |
| Workpc | Managed `127.0.0.1` SSH forward with automatic startup and recovery | Independent of VS Code sessions and never bound to all interfaces |
| Test beds | Dedicated Linux and Windows development identities | Existing production-connected identities remain unchanged |
| Data | Empty database plus deterministic synthetic seed | No production credentials, enrollments, sessions, or personal data |
| Promotion | Exact development-tested commit | Production backup and practical rollback before migration-bearing promotion |

