## Readiness Gate Transfer

1. `G1-DEVELOPMENT-ISOLATION` — development owns every writable runtime and configuration surface,
   and production remains unchanged.
2. `G2-DEVELOPMENT-COMMUNICATION` — dedicated development projects complete a manual handshake and
   maintain bounded reporting without redirecting production identities.
3. `G3-PRIVATE-ACCESS` — test beds use the LAN endpoint, Workpc uses a localhost-only tunnel, and
   development has no public route.
4. `G4-PROMOTION-RECOVERY` — exact-commit promotion, non-production migration/recovery evidence,
   required client/browser checks, production backup, health, and rollback all pass.

## Constraints

- Plain HTTP is an accepted development-only LAN choice; production HTTPS policy is not weakened.
- Development credentials, TOTP material, cookies, and sessions are unique and never reused in
  production.
- Restart and deployment commands must scope explicitly to the development Compose project.
- CPU and memory limits protect production from routine development load.
- The exact development root and Workpc tunnel mechanism may be selected during implementation,
  but neither choice may weaken the transferred gates.
- `work/tool-shed.yaml` remains `combined` until a real, verified development target exists.

## Non-Goals

- A second physical host, VM, Docker daemon, or public development hostname.
- Email, webhook, or hosted completion-notification delivery.
- Copying production data, identities, credentials, sessions, or reporter state.
- Automatic deployment on every push before the development contract is proven.
- Any production change during the Plan or Roadmap cycles.

